{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["The reason for <b>YHWH your Elohim you shall fear</b>: since after He commanded concerning love, He mentions fear of Him, so that one will not sin and be punished." "<b>And Him you shall serve</b> — to do all that He has commanded you, like a servant who observes the commandments of his master. And it is possible that this is a hint to the sacrificial service for His name, and thus they said in the Sifrei (see 96) on the verse <i>After YHWH your Elohim you shall walk, and Him you shall fear, and His commandments you shall keep, and to His voice you shall hearken, and Him you shall serve</i> (Deuteronomy 13:5): \"Serve\" — in His Torah; \"serve\" — in His sanctuary. The meaning is that after He warned concerning the observance of all the commandments and hearkening to His voice, <b>and Him you shall serve</b> will refer to serving Him in the sanctuary with sacrifices, song, and prostrations there, for this is called <i>avodah</i> (service), as He said (Numbers 18:7) <i>the service of a gift I give your priesthood</i>, and He said (ibid. verse 23) <i>and the Levite shall serve</i>. And they further expounded <b>serve in His Torah</b> — to learn Torah and meditate upon it, and it too is service before Him; accordingly, <b>and Him you shall serve</b> also with sacrifices. And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Deuteronomy 6:13): <b>YHWH your Elohim you shall fear</b> — that you shall not transgress negative commandments; <b>and Him you shall serve</b> — in positive commandments. And this is not correct, because the other verse states <i>and Him you shall fear, and His commandments you shall keep, and Him you shall serve</i>." "<b>And in His name you shall swear</b> — it is not a commandment to swear, but it is a warning that only in His name shall you swear, not in the name of another el. Therefore it is written after it <b>you shall not go after other elohim</b> — that you shall not go after them in any matter at all: you shall not fear them, you shall not serve them, and you shall not swear in their name. But in the Gemara of tractate Temurah they expounded it concerning an oath of truth that is permitted; they said: \"'In His name you shall swear' — why do I need it? If it does not apply to the oath of judges, which is derived from <i>the oath of YHWH shall be between them both</i> (Exodus 22:10), apply it to oaths that are permitted in the world; and if it does not apply to an oath of a commandment, which is derived from <i>and in Him you shall cleave</i> (Deuteronomy 13:5), apply it to a voluntary oath.\" And I saw further in Tanḥuma: The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: Do not suppose that it is permitted to you to swear in My name even in truth; you are not permitted to swear in My name unless all these qualities are in you — <b>YHWH your Elohim you shall fear</b>, that you be like those who were called God-fearers: Abraham, Joseph, Job; <b>and Him you shall serve</b>, that you set yourself apart for Torah and commandments, and have no other service; therefore it says <b>and Him you shall serve and in Him you shall cleave</b> — now is it possible for a person to cleave to the Shekhinah? Behold it is already stated (Deuteronomy 4:24), <i>for YHWH your Elohim is a consuming fire</i>! Rather, it comes to tell you: anyone who marries off his daughter to a disciple who reads and studies, and does business for him, and benefits him from his property — this is what is meant by <b>and in Him you shall cleave</b>. If you have all these qualities, you are permitted to swear; and if not, you are not permitted to swear. This is the language of that aggadah up to here. Now according to their understanding, the meaning of the verse <b>and in His name you shall swear</b> is a permission that He allows us to swear in His name in a manner of honor — as if He said: <b>YHWH your Elohim you shall fear</b>, but <b>in His name you shall swear</b> and you need not fear this; and because He placed this permission after fear and service, they expounded that there is no permission for an oath except after all these qualities. Or one may say: <b>YHWH your Elohim you shall fear</b>, and let your fear of Him be so great before you that His name becomes for you an instrument of oath — for even when you wish to fulfill any matter, you swear in His name to do harm and do not change. And the meaning of <b>and Him you shall serve</b> according to their opinion is that you should be to Him at all times like a purchased servant who ministers before his master constantly, doing his master's work as primary and his own needs as secondary, to the point that from this emerges what they said (Avot 2:12), <i>let all your deeds be for the sake of Heaven</i> — that even the needs of his body should be for the sake of the service of El: he eats and sleeps and tends to his needs in order to sustain the body to serve YHWH, in the manner of what they said (Genesis Rabbah 9:6): <i>and behold, it was very good</i> (Genesis 1:31) — this is sleep; now is sleep good? Rather, since he sleeps a little, he rises and engages in Torah. And all bodily needs are intended for the verse that is written (Psalms 146:2): <i>I will praise YHWH while I live; I will sing to my Elohim</i>, etc. And this interpretation is correct."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {2 "In the Tanḥuma passage, the phrase לְהָרַע וְלֹא יָמִיר is rendered hyper-literally ('to do harm and does not change'); the idiom may mean 'to carry out [the sworn intent] and not deviate.' The precise sense is uncertain and the passage may reflect a textual variant."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:15.827144684Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}